ADMINISTRATOR SIGN-IN (MULTI-FACTOR) AND THE UNDER-DEVELOPMENT FACE NOTICE
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Administrator sign-in verifies the password and any enrolled security factors.
If a passkey or hand-pattern lock is enrolled, it must be completed. The final
verification step can use the enabled face flow or an emailed one-time code;
when an authenticator app is enrolled, the administrator can choose its
time-based code instead of email. A factor is only asked for when it has
actually been registered.

To use Google Authenticator on two phones, sign in as the administrator and
open Admin > Security Centre > Authenticator app. Confirm the current
administrator password to start setup. Scan the same QR code on both phones
before entering a current six-digit code to confirm enrollment. Codes rotate
every 30 seconds; either enrolled phone can generate a valid code. Keep the
phones secured and do not share or publish the QR code or setup key. Replacing
or removing the authenticator invalidates the old setup. If no authenticator
is enrolled, the configured email code remains the final-step option.

Live face capture is currently marked UNDER DEVELOPMENT: the camera check has a
known problem that is being fixed. While it is paused the sign-in screen shows
an editable notice instead of the camera. Selecting the notice's OK button
finishes the sign-in, but only after the password and every other enabled factor
above have already passed — OK does not authenticate anyone by itself and it
never replaces the password. The camera is still one click away from the notice
("Use live face capture camera instead") if you want to show it.

Both outcomes are audited. Every attempt — the OK path, the camera path, a
success and a blocked attempt — is written to Administrator > Security Centre >
Admin Security & Login History with its exact date and time, sign-in status and
the verification method used, and the configured administrator address receives
the alert email through the configured provider (a captured photograph is
attached when one exists).

The notice heading, the notice message and the "OK may continue" switch are
edited in Administrator > Security Centre > Administrator sign-in notice. They
are stored in the settings table and read live by the sign-in screen, so a
wording change appears on the very next sign-in with no rebuild. If OK-to-
continue is switched off, the notice is still shown but an administrator must
switch it back on before sign-in can be completed through it. Turn the first
switch off to bring live face capture back; the wording is kept for later.

THE HIDDEN PROJECT GUIDE PORTAL (OPENS FROM THE REGIONAL OFFICE SIGN-IN)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The Project Guide portal is deliberately discreet. It is not listed on the
homepage and it cannot be reached by changing the address bar: the route
refuses anyone who is not signed in as the guide, so guessing URLs shows
nothing. The only doorway is the Regional Office sign-in page:

  1. On the homepage choose the Regional Office sign-in, or open
     http://localhost:3000/?portal=regional
  2. Type 6 into the small, intentionally invisible field that sits below the
     password box, and press Enter. The full phrase "no _____6____" also works,
     in any spacing. Typing this reveals a page only — it authenticates nobody.
  3. The separate Project Guide sign-in page opens. Enter the guide credentials
     from the list above:
       guide.tabassum@schoolos.demo (usr_guide_001) + the shared demo password
  4. Credentials are verified on the server against the project_guide_accounts
     registry. A wrong email or password is rejected exactly like any other
     sign-in, and a non-guide account is refused with 403.

Inside, the Project Guide can:
  - Follow a review progress summary — "x of 5 pages reviewed", how many need
    attention and how many are still unchecked, with a progress bar.
  - Filter the checklist with All pages / To check / Needs attention / Reviewed
    chips, each showing a live count, so only the relevant pages are listed.
  - Review & check published pages — open each live page (public home,
    Regional Office sign-in, Student sign-in, Teacher sign-in, User guide),
    write a short note, and record "Mark reviewed" or "Needs attention". The
    page can only ever be one of that fixed list, and the decision, the note
    and the exact time are stored and added to the activity log.
  - Clear a recorded decision ("Clear decision") to return a page to the
    "not checked" state and check it again.
  - Copy summary — copies the whole checklist (status, notes and counts) as
    plain text for a report, without exposing anything else.
  - Keep mentor observations — add, edit and remove their own private notes
    about the project. These stay inside the portal and every change is logged.
  - Plan project work — create, edit, filter and delete private tasks and
    milestones; set status, priority and due date, and review overdue or
    completed work. Tasks are scoped to the signed-in guide and do not change
    school records or grant school-administration permissions.
  - Edit the project description and the photograph of the Project Guide's own
    public profile, which updates the Leadership & Project Mentorship section
    on the homepage.
  - View the approved project settings and read the full activity log of every
    change made from the portal.
It cannot read passwords, API keys or encryption secrets, cannot edit other
pages, cannot administer the school, and is entirely separate from Regional
Office permissions. To finish a demonstration, use Sign out.

2. PRESENTATION SCRIPT AND RECOMMENDED WALKTHROUGH
-------------------------------------------------

One-minute opening:
"Imagine a school day where a student can find the right class resource, a
teacher can follow up on learning, and a parent can see information linked to
their child—all in one connected school workspace. SchoolOS AI brings those
role-specific journeys together. I will show what works in this deployment,
where AI can help, and where people and school approval remain essential."

Three-to-five-minute visitor-first walkthrough:
  1. Homepage: invite the audience to choose a role card. Show that each opens
     the matching sign-in role, then point out the public user guide and school
     registration path. Open the floating "Ask SchoolOS" helper and ask a
     platform question to show the signed-out answer is limited to public
     product guidance. Ask: "Which part of a school day would you want to
     make easier?"
  2. Student: follow one realistic journey—open a class resource, find an
     assignment or quiz, then check personal attendance or marks. Try an AI
     study tool only if a configured provider is ready.
  3. Teacher: show assigned classes and one real workflow such as attendance,
     marks, a resource or an assignment. Explain assignment-based access. If
     AI is enabled, generate a draft and review it before use.
  4. Parent: show the linked learner's available attendance, marks, homework
     and progress. Emphasize that unrelated learners are not visible.
  5. Principal and Regional Office: compare school-level coordination with
     region-scoped oversight. Use fictional records only.
  6. School Store: first, as the platform administrator, open Admin > School
     Store > Official managers and create a separate account using the
     manager's name, login email and unique store-only password. Share the
     password securely. Sign out and sign in at `/storeweb` with that official
     account to review orders across schools and update fulfillment or stock.
     Then, as a principal, place a fictional reader or retail order and open
     My school orders to review item details, payment state and fulfillment
     updates. Use Refresh tracking after the official manager updates the
     order. Never use a school principal account as the official manager.
  7. Administrator: sign in as the administrator and let the audience watch the
     factor chain complete — password, then either the editable under-
     development face-capture notice and its OK button, or the live camera.
     Open Admin Security & Login History immediately afterwards to show that
     attempt recorded with its date, time, status and method. Then open AI &
     Smart Features and show the 15 centrally accessible modules, Feature
     Control and provider readiness. If configured, demonstrate Certificate &
     Letter Management using an existing template: choose student and school,
     review the filled fields/PDF, then explicitly approve before applying the
     configured signature and issuing. Finish in Appearance to show full design
     presets across the site and role portals, and in Leadership & Project
     Mentorship to show that the public founder/guide section is editable.
  7. Close by asking which role journey mattered most and invite visitors to
     choose their role or request school onboarding from the homepage.

For a six-to-eight-minute presentation, let an audience member choose the role
journey, then follow the matching path above. Keep a short path ready:
homepage role cards → student resource → teacher follow-up → parent view →
administrator controls. Do not sign in to real learner accounts publicly.

To prepare additional fictional records for the 15 centrally managed features,
run this additive command from the project root:
  npm run demo:showcase
It adds 36 clearly marked [DEMO] records and acknowledgement examples to the
dedicated SchoolOS Demo Academy. It can be run repeatedly: existing records are
left unchanged and nothing is deleted or reset. The dedicated demo school and
its fictional accounts must already exist. Run this only on a demo database;
do not run it against a database containing real school data.

For a genuine live video demonstration, sign in as the teacher and student in
two separate browser profiles or devices. Permit camera and microphone access.
The live class uses browser media/WebRTC; it is not a YouTube stream. A single
browser session cannot demonstrate a second participant.

Theme: "Artificial Intelligence for a Better Life"
  - Access: role-specific learning and school tools are available in one web
    application.
  - Time: AI can help draft explanations, notes, quizzes and study plans.
  - Human judgment: AI results are advisory. Teachers and authorized leaders
    review content and own official decisions.
  - Privacy: only use fictional demo information. The server applies account,
    role, school, class and parent-child access checks to supported workflows.
  - Honesty: a configured integration is not the same as a successful live
    workflow. Demonstrate a real response before describing it as working.
  - Engagement: ask visitors to choose the role or workflow most relevant to
    them. Let the interface answer that question instead of presenting every
    feature as a long list.

3. FEATURE GUIDE FOR ALL SIX PORTALS
------------------------------------
The portal navigation is the left-side menu after sign-in. Select a feature
there; the current screen opens in the main content area. Available entries
may vary with role, school assignment and administrator Feature Control. Every
signed-in portal also carries the floating "Ask SchoolOS" help assistant in the
bottom corner; it answers platform questions using only that role's permitted
knowledge scope (see section 4).

