4. CROSS-PORTAL FEATURES AND HOW DATA MOVES
-------------------------------------------
Accounts and permissions: users sign in with an assigned role. The server
authenticates the account, checks the role and applicable school/class/region
or parent-child relationship, then returns only authorized data. Hiding a
navigation item is not the security boundary; server routes also check access.

Teaching assignments: a principal creates class/section/subject assignments.
These determine which classes and subjects a teacher can manage. A class
teacher assignment grants the class-level workflow; subject teachers are
limited to their assigned subjects/classes.

Attendance and marks: teachers record attendance and marks for assigned
classes. Students see their own records, parents see linked learners, and
principals review school-level records. The roles do not share identical views.

Learning resources and uploads: teachers share class resources and supported
quizzes/videos. Students access resources available to their class. Uploads are
kept outside the public web root and protected download routes check access.
Current documented limits: 25 MB for ordinary learning files; video lessons
up to 250 MB; notice media up to 60 MB; profile photos up to 8 MB. Registration
evidence files are up to 10 MB each.

Forms, notices, calendar and meetings: creators select a supported audience;
the application saves submissions/events and returns them to permitted
participants. Private video meetings require both participants online and
camera/microphone permission.

AI request flow: a signed-in user submits a request to a server endpoint. The
server checks role policy, request limits and configured provider, prepares
permitted context, calls the selected external provider, then returns the
response. Prompts/context are sent to that provider for provider-backed
features. AI can be wrong; review output. The AI does not automatically
publish official marks, approve registrations, or make disciplinary decisions.

Ask SchoolOS help assistant ("Nova"): the floating "Ask SchoolOS" helper in the
bottom corner answers questions about the platform itself, not about school
subjects. It is deliberately role-aware. The server builds each answer from one
internal knowledge base (app/lib/schoolos-knowledge.ts) that tags every entry
with the roles allowed to hear it, so the brief handed to the model contains
only that account's own scope: a student is told about student tools and never
about administrator screens, and a principal is never told about platform-wide
administration. Public platform facts are the same for everyone (what SchoolOS
is, the six portals, how to sign in, school registration, the notice board and
public forms, the founder and project guide, and the general AI approach).
Hard limits are repeated to the model on every request, so the assistant will
never reveal or guess a password, a one-time reset or activation link, a
recovery code, an API key or a database path; will never describe or confirm a
hidden entry route or repeat the secret phrase; will never share another
person's records, marks, attendance or login activity; will never claim to have
changed a record, approved a registration or sent a message; and will never
invent a feature, price or policy. If a question falls outside the brief it says
so and points to the school administrator. A signed-out visitor gets the public
topic answers only, with no account or school data. "Send to admin for review"
turns the question into a help request in Administrator > Help requests — the
assistant itself never changes anything.

Live-class attention monitoring: the teacher enables monitoring on a
particular scheduled/live class. The student receives a monitoring notice and
must grant camera permission where the feature requests it. Where supported,
computer-vision analysis runs in the student's browser; only event metadata
(not camera recordings or screenshots) is sent to the server. The teacher
panel groups possible drowsiness/prolonged looking away, face unavailable or
covered, and possible phone detections, with student name and timestamp.
Cooldowns reduce repeated notifications. The teacher reviews an event; a
detection is not proof of misconduct. Cross-origin Jitsi video frames may not
be readable by the SchoolOS page, so attention analysis can depend on a
separately accessible camera stream and available browser/model assets. Never
promise that Jitsi's embedded stream is always accessible to computer vision.

Interface language: the language selector is a personal choice, not a platform
setting. The choice is kept in the visitor's own browser and its own cookie, so
changing it changes only that person's view — it never changes the language for
other users, other roles or the shared website content. The default is English,
the home page always starts in English for a new visitor, and any text that has
not been translated falls back to English rather than showing a blank.

Portal identity and navigation: each of the six portals (administrator,
regional office, principal, teacher, student, parent) carries its own accent
colour and header/sidebar treatment, so the signed-in role is obvious at a
glance. The Overview of every portal opens with a compact identity banner in
that role's accent — a tagline (for example "TEACH · TRACK · SUPPORT"), the
portal name, a one-line description of what the workspace is for, and three
focus chips — so each of the six workspaces reads as its own place rather than
the same screen in a different colour. Every navigation button in the portal
sidebar is the same height, width, padding, icon slot and font size — including
the AI & smart feature entries — and each one starts with a real icon rather
than a stray arrow; a long entry name is shortened with an ellipsis instead of
making one button larger than the others. Every feature-opening button on a
homepage card (the "Enter as …" buttons and the "Explore … portal" buttons) also
shares one pill shape and one height.

Opened feature areas: every feature you open fills the full width of the portal
content area — the strip of empty space that used to sit to the right of a
feature panel is gone, and the panels are the same width as the top bar. Each
feature header is underlined by a hairline with a short accent segment in that
portal's own colour, so a wide panel still reads as one designed sheet, and long
paragraphs keep a comfortable reading measure instead of stretching across the
screen.

Homepage layout: the six role portals are shown as three comfortable cards per
row (two rows) instead of five cramped columns. Each card carries its own role
accent on the top edge, a gradient icon tile in that accent, a soft corner glow,
a small positional number, a clearly separated highlight line and an accent
"Enter as …" pill; the "More than a notice board" tiles lift slightly on hover.
All six portal cards are also editable without touching code: in Administrator >
Website Content & CMS > Live Web Editor, select the "Portal cards" section and
rewrite each card's heading, description and highlight line (six sets of
fields). The role each card opens and its icon stay fixed, so an edit can never
point a card at the wrong portal.

Public notices: the homepage notice area lists the published notices as
equal-height cards in a responsive grid with the publisher, title, body, date
and any attachment shown on the card, plus a notice count and a refresh button.
When nothing has been published yet, the area shows an explanatory empty state
instead of a collapsed or broken-looking section.

School Store ordering and fulfillment: the storefront uses the published
catalogue and the payment methods enabled by the platform. A principal orders
for their own school and can see only orders they personally placed for that
school. Their order view shows line items, quantities, total, payment method
and status, fulfillment status and the SchoolOS manager update; Refresh tracking
reloads it. The principal cannot confirm, cancel or fulfill orders. The
principal can access the school's reader/card controls for devices and RFID
cards provisioned after an official manager confirms an attendance-reader
order.

Available checkout choices are cash at school, direct UPI (when a school UPI
ID is configured), and Razorpay online checkout (when the administrator has
configured and enabled the gateway). Razorpay may offer card, UPI,
netbanking or wallet methods according to merchant account and gateway
availability; BHIM, Paytm, PhonePe, Google Pay, Navi and other apps are not
each separately integrated by SchoolOS. Direct UPI is a manual transfer and
reference workflow, not automatic payment confirmation.

`/storeweb` is operated by official SchoolOS store staff, not a school manager.
Only a platform administrator can create this separate account from Admin >
School Store > Official managers by entering a name, unique login email and
store-only password (minimum 12 characters). The password is saved as a salted
hash and cannot be recovered or viewed; the administrator can set a new one.
Disabling an account or resetting its password revokes its active sessions.
The official manager reviews and processes orders across schools, records
fulfillment notes, and updates shared retail stock. This account cannot access
school staff accounts, school settings or payment gateway credentials.

Payment and order safeguards: cash-at-school orders remain pending until the
official manager records cash as received. For direct UPI orders, the buyer
submits a transaction reference and the manager must verify it before marking
paid. Razorpay card/online payment status is set by server-side gateway
signature verification; the manager cannot manually mark an unverified card
payment as paid. Only unpaid COD orders without a submitted payment reference
can be cancelled from `/storeweb`; paid orders cannot be cancelled there.
Fulfillment follows the placed → confirmed → fulfilled flow, and unpaid online
orders cannot be fulfilled. Gateway secrets and payment configuration remain
administrator-only. The official manager also has a server-persisted packing
checklist and shipment record. Each order line can be checked as packed; packing
cannot be completed until every line is checked. The manager can record a
carrier, tracking/LR number and dispatch note, then advance shipment tracking
through packed, dispatched and delivered. Dispatch requires fulfillment and any
required online payment confirmation. Principals see the shipment status,
carrier, tracking number, dispatch/delivery times and manager update on their
own order.

The `/storeweb` operations hub provides 20+ working controls:
  1. Six live summary cards for queued orders, payment review, dispatch readiness,
     in-transit/delivered counts, verified paid order value and stock alerts.
  2. Search across order number, school, buyer, role, item, payment method and
     submitted payment reference.
  3. Filter orders by order status and payment status.
  4. Filter orders by cash, UPI or gateway payment method.
  5. Restrict results to today, the last seven days or the last 30 days.
  6. Sort newest-first, oldest-first or by order value.
  7. Clear all order filters in one action.
  8. Export the currently filtered orders as CSV.
  9. Copy an order number or submitted payment reference.
 10. Review school, buyer, role, line items, quantities, totals and buyer notes.
 11. Record and edit an internal fulfillment note.
 12. Confirm eligible COD cash receipt.
 13. Verify a submitted direct-UPI reference.
 14. Confirm, fulfill or cancel only when the payment/order rules allow it.
 15. Use a server-saved per-item packing checklist.
 16. Prevent packing completion while any order line remains unchecked.
 17. Save courier/transport provider and a tracking or LR number.
 18. Save dispatch and handover notes.
 19. Advance shipment tracking from packed to dispatched to delivered.
 20. Require valid fulfillment/payment state before dispatch.
 21. Record dispatch and delivery timestamps for the principal's tracking view.
 22. Search shared retail stock by item name or category.
 23. Filter low stock and out-of-stock items separately.
 24. Apply one-unit stock increases/decreases or save an exact validated count.
 25. Export the visible inventory list as CSV.
 26. Refresh all order, shipment and inventory data from the server and show the
     refresh time.
 These are workflow controls, not integrations with courier networks: carrier
 status is entered by the manager and is not automatically fetched from a
 third-party transport API. Inter-school transfer automation is not included.

5. SETUP-DEPENDENT FEATURES AND HONEST DEMO NOTES
------------------------------------------------
AI: an administrator must configure and enable a supported provider in
Administrator > AI & API Management. Supported provider credentials are
server-only. If no provider is configured, the app should report configuration
is missing; do not present a canned answer as a live AI response.

AI Website Manager: needs server access to the project files and configured AI
support. Review its proposed diff/summary, backup and validation result before
confirming a significant change. A deployed application without writable source
files cannot apply code changes. Portal feature manager creates configured
pages and is not a replacement for source-code editing.

AI live-class attention: requires an enabled per-class teacher switch,
student-facing notice/consent, camera access, supported browser/model assets,
and an active class. The teacher's saved alert sounds are shared from the
administrator manager. Verify each sound and notification in a real class
before claiming that computer vision works on a particular Jitsi deployment.

Jitsi/WebRTC: camera/microphone generally require HTTPS or localhost. Network
firewalls can block peer media; the host may need TURN configuration via
SCHOOL_OS_TURN_URLS and SCHOOL_OS_TURN_SHARED_SECRET. Use two participants and
check the browser permission prompts.

Administrator live face capture: needs camera permission, HTTPS or localhost,
the bundled browser/face-model assets and a working server-side inference path.
It is currently marked UNDER DEVELOPMENT from Administrator > Security Centre,
so the sign-in screen shows the notice and OK-to-continue instead. While the
camera is paused, say so plainly — do not present the paused check as a working
face-recognition demo. What you can demonstrate honestly is the notice wording
being edited live from Security Centre and the resulting sign-in attempt
appearing in Admin Security & Login History.

Database/Supabase: the project currently retains SQLite as its application
database. Optional Supabase connection settings do not automatically migrate
or delete SQLite data. Cloud integration is not complete until the environment
is configured, versioned SQL migrations are applied, RLS/access policies are
verified, and a safe test record is successfully written and read back.

Email/observability/search integrations: configuring a provider connection or
passing its connection test does not create every possible product workflow.
For example, an integration test does not itself create a document indexing
pipeline or automatically send every notice by email. Report only workflows
that are present and verified.

Android builds: signed APK generation requires a persistent Node.js host,
public HTTPS URL, Java 21+, Android SDK 36+, generated Android/Gradle wrapper,
and protected persistent release-keystore settings. The Android app display
name is SchoolOS AI Explorer. An administrator can upload a signed platform
APK or create one with the server build service. After publication, visitors
can directly download the latest APK from the homepage or `/explorer`; the
page shows a not-yet-published message until a platform release exists.
Administrators can separately queue a download announcement to every active
account with a valid email address after reviewing the recipient count and
confirming the send. Email provider acceptance is not proof of inbox delivery;
review the job status and Email Centre audit log. School-specific builds are
not exposed on the public page. Students, parents, teachers and staff can use
the mobile-ready web app at `/?source=mobile` even before the APK is published.
The Android wrapper opens the same SchoolOS web app and uses the same
server/database; it is not an offline database. A local localhost URL cannot be
shared to a phone. An APK has not been built or published just by updating this
guide; confirm a successful upload/build in Administrator > Mobile App Links
and test the public download on a separate Android device.

6. JUDGE QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS
------------------------------
Q: What problem does SchoolOS solve?
A: It brings role-appropriate learning, attendance, results, assignments,
   notices, calendars and family/school communication into a single portal.

Q: What does AI do here?
A: Configured AI tools can tutor, summarize, draft notes/quizzes/timetables and
   offer role-aware guidance. People review results; AI does not take official
   school actions by itself.

Q: Does AI make decisions about a student's conduct or authenticity of school
   documents?
A: No. Live-class detections are possible events requiring teacher review.
   Document analysis can flag a possible concern, but staff must compare source
   evidence and verify independently; the system cannot prove originality.

Q: Is AI guaranteed to be correct?
A: No. Generative output can be wrong. Check school facts and educational
   content with official records or a teacher.

Q: Does AI read every school document?
A: Not by default. Some prompts use limited role-authorized context. A full
   document extraction, permission-aware ingestion, search and cited retrieval
   pipeline should not be claimed unless it is implemented and verified.

Q: Does connecting an external service mean it is used throughout the app?
A: No. A connection test verifies a specific API interaction. Separate
   product workflows may still need implementation and testing.

Q: Can the "Ask SchoolOS" assistant be tricked into revealing a password or
   telling a student about administrator screens?
A: No, by design. Its knowledge base tags every entry with the roles allowed to
   hear it, and only that role's slice is sent to the model, so a student is
   never given administrator or other people's information. Hard limits are
   repeated on every request: it never gives or guesses a password, reset link,
   recovery code, API key or database path; never reveals a hidden entry route;
   never shares another person's records; never claims to have changed
   anything; and never invents a feature, price or policy. Out-of-scope
   questions are declined and passed to the school administrator.

Q: How does a live class work?
A: Teacher and students join from their browsers. Live audio/video uses WebRTC
   and requires both participants online, permissions and a network that allows
   media traffic. Restrictive networks may require TURN relay configuration.

Q: What does the School Digital Twin mean?
A: Where presented, it is an aggregate summary of available school data such
   as enrollment, attendance, marks or assignments. It is not a 3D campus or
   sensor-connected simulation.

Q: What database is used?
A: The existing application uses server-side SQLite by default. Optional
   Supabase work does not mean data has moved to the cloud. A cloud migration
   must be performed deliberately, with backups and verified migrations.

Q: Who manages `/storeweb`, and what can a principal see?
A: `/storeweb` is restricted to separate official SchoolOS store-manager
   accounts created by the platform administrator; school principals cannot
   create or use those accounts. The official manager handles cross-school
   order fulfillment and retail stock. A principal can place orders for their
   school and see their own order's items, payment details and current
   fulfillment status in School Store > My school orders. Principals cannot
   fulfill orders or see another principal's orders.

Q: Can an administrator use Google Authenticator on two phones?
A: Yes. In Admin > Security Centre, start Authenticator app setup and scan the
   same QR code on both phones before confirming enrollment with a current
   six-digit code. Either phone can then provide the 30-second sign-in code.
   Keep both devices and the setup key secure; replacing/removing the
   authenticator invalidates the previous setup.

Q: What learning support is available for CWSN learners?
A: Student learning-access aids include display/text scaling, font and spacing
   choices, contrast/dark/grayscale modes, clearer focus and links, reduced
   motion, a simplified layout, reading ruler and browser text-to-speech. A
   caption-availability control and keyboard tips are also present. These
   optional aids are not a complete screen reader or live caption service,
   and do not replace individualized accommodations; support varies by device
   and browser.

Q: Who controls school logos and school portal appearance?
A: A principal can manage branding and appearance for their own school. The
   logo and school-scoped theme apply to that school's teacher, student and
   parent experience. A platform administrator can manage school branding
   across approved schools and controls the platform-wide appearance.

Q: What happens to uploaded files?
A: They are stored on the server outside the public web root and are delivered
   through protected routes where applicable. The host operator must protect
   storage and backups.

Q: Why does the administrator sign-in show an "under development" message?
A: The live face-capture check has a known problem that is being fixed, so the
   administrator paused it from Security Centre. The sign-in still requires the
   password and every other enabled factor; the notice's OK button only finishes
   an already-verified sign-in. Each attempt is still logged with its date, time,
   status and method in Admin Security & Login History, and the alert email is
   still sent. The wording of that notice is itself editable by the
   administrator, and the check is switched back on with one setting.

Q: Does the language selector change the whole website for everybody?
A: No. The chosen language is stored in the individual visitor's browser and its
   own cookie, so it affects only that person. Other users, other roles and the
   shared website content keep their own language, the default is English, and
   untranslated text falls back to English.

Q: Are the role portals identical?
A: They share the same layout system but each of the six portals has its own
   accent colour and header/sidebar treatment, its own navigation entries and
   its own access scope, so a principal, teacher, student, parent and regional
   officer never see the same workspace.

Q: How are passwords and sessions handled?
A: Passwords are stored as scrypt hashes with salts. Sessions use an HttpOnly,
   SameSite cookie and the server stores a token hash. Production requires
   HTTPS and secure host configuration. No claim here replaces a security audit.

Q: What is not complete?
A: Anything explicitly marked setup-dependent above requires its provider,
   permissions, host tooling or live workflow test. The app should be judged on
   what can be demonstrated in this build, not on a roadmap or label alone.

7. PRIVACY, SECURITY AND RESET NOTES
------------------------------------
  - Use fictional demo records only. Never show real learner names, marks,
    contact information, health information or disciplinary information.
  - Do not expose passwords, API keys, encryption keys, signing keys or the
    server environment file during a presentation.
  - Do not claim a model detection is proof, a forecast is certain, or AI output
    is an official school decision.
  - Production needs HTTPS, protected server storage, backups, updates,
    monitoring and independent security testing.
  - Uploaded student camera frames are not intended to be saved as recordings
    or screenshots by the attention feature; event metadata is sent for the
    teacher's review.

DEMO RESET (DESTRUCTIVE)
------------------------
  npm run demo:reset
This replaces fictional school, principal, teacher, student, parent, attendance,
materials, notices and related demo records. Existing administrator accounts
and non-AI website settings are preserved according to the reset script. It
generates a new shared demo password and updates this combined guide. Do not run
this against a database containing real or valuable records. Back up first.

Use only fictional demo accounts and data on a public demo server.

